UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 1, Lesson 1: Identity and influences', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and values. Year 7 students examine influences without ranking people’s worth, and practise respectful language about difference.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nList four influences on a fictional Year 7 student’s identity and explain one helpful and one risky message among them.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: influences specific; helpful/risky messages distinguished without shaming whole groups.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTreating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently create an influence map for yourself or a persona, write a five-sentence reflection on one choice you can control, and note one trusted adult you could talk with if a message feels unsafe.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and values. Year 7 students examine influences without ranking people’s worth, and practise respectful language about difference.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PD4-1"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently create an influence map for yourself or a persona, write a five-sentence reflection on one choice you can control, and note one trusted adult you could talk with if a message feels unsafe. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using identity on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.. Correction: Strong answer: influences specific;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating identity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with identity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about identity with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for identity. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for identity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","influences specific; helpful/risky messages distinguished without shaming whole groups.","Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"List four influences on a fictional Year 7 student’s identity and explain one helpful and one risky message among them. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for identity; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and","influences specific; helpful/risky messages distinguished without shaming whole groups.","Careful use of influence with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named influence, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","Name influence only, without linking it to identity.","Apply identity to new material and name the influence used."],"question":"Which response best applies identity with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on identity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety.","Use a put-down and call it honesty.","Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","influences specific; helpful/risky messages distinguished without shaming whole groups."],"question":"Which response best evaluates safe, healthy or respectful choices when analysing identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety.","Use a put-down and call it honesty.","Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with identity."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety.","Use a put-down and call it honesty.","Example: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media).","Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Independently create an influence map for yourself or a persona, write a five-sentence reflection on one choice you can","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.","Name influence only, with no linked example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies identity to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about identity with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating identity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using identity on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for identity. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with identity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences.. Correction: Strong answer: influences specific;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-pdhpe-y7-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise identity.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use identity with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify influence and explain why it matters.","I can practise values independently on a new prompt.","I can notice respect needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and values.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media). Claim: peer and media messages can pull in different directions; naming them helps Jordan choose which advice to test.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList four influences on a fictional Year 7 student’s identity and explain one helpful and one risky message among them.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: influences specific; helpful/risky messages distinguished without shaming whole groups.","explain":"Identity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and values. Year 7 students examine influences without ranking people’s worth, and practise respectful language about difference.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Identity influences.","caption":"Respect difference.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Influences</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">peers/media/family</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Choice</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">test advice</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media). Claim: peer and m Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media). Claim: peer and m Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media). Claim: peer and media messages can pull in different directions; naming them helps Jordan choose which advice to test. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Sort messages.","caption":"No group shame.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Helpful</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">message</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risky</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">message</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTreating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media). Claim: peer and media messages can pull in different directions; naming them helps Jordan choose which advice to test.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: influences specific; helpful/risky messages distinguished without shaming whole groups.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Treating identity as fixed or mocking someone’s influences. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about identity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIdentity is shaped by many influences (family, friends, media, culture, place) and can include roles, strengths and values. Year 7 students examine influences without ranking people’s worth, and practise respectful language about difference. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 1, Lesson 1: Identity and influences","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Identity, health and wellbeing","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Jordan maps influences — basketball club (peers), older cousin (advice), gaming chat (media). Claim: peer and media messages can pull in different directions; naming them helps Jordan choose which advice to test.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Identity influences.","caption":"Respect difference.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" heig